In this lesson, you will learn about nouns and articles. *Nouns* are words that refer to things, people, animals, places, etc. In both English and Dutch, nouns are often preceded by an *article*. In English, there are the *definite article* _the_ and the *indefinite articles* _a_ and _an_. In Dutch, there are two definite articles (_de_ and _het_) and one indefinite article (_een_).

^de or het?^
While English has only one *definite article* (_the_), Dutch has two: _de_ and _het_. Which one you have to use depends on the noun: _de auto_ (_the car_) - _het vliegtuig_ (_the airplane_). While there are no clear-cut rules for determining which nouns go with _de_ and which go with _het_, there are some tips and tricks to figure out the article for some words, which will be introduced throughout the course as they become relevant. 

The first tip is that almost all nouns referring to people and professions go with _de_: _de man_ (_the man_), _de vrouw_ (_the woman_), _de zanger_ (_the singer_), _de leraar_ (_the teacher_), and so on. *However*, there are two notable exceptions to this: _het meisje_ (_the girl_) and _het kind_ (_the child/kid_). 

For the rest of the words in this lesson, you will just have to remember which nouns go with which article. It is good practice to always learn new nouns together with their article!

^een^
Luckily, Dutch only has one *indefinite article*: _een_. So it doesn't matter whether a noun goes with the definite article _de_ or _het_, you always use _een_: _een auto_ (_a car_) - _een vliegtuig_ (_an airplane_). 